 again the edge wells are shaded in red  we omit that data  we have our positive and negative  controls on both sides of the plate  and then our dose response screen was  performed at  nine concentrations shown by the gold to  green gradient  for every compound so we tested  um we tested all 335 compounds from 10  micromolar down to one nanomolar  in triplicate and again performed the  drug screen  used used three different donors blood  within a within a triplicate  these are some of the raw data from  our dose response screen these are  sickling  for each of these three compounds i'm  showing sickling kinetics at 10  micromolar  the continuous curves again are the  single well kinetics  and the dashed curves with error bars  are the positive and negative controls  we got sickling kinetics for obviously  for  all nine concentrations for every  compound and  to better compare what was happening at  each concentration  we plotted the v1 of the compound  relative to the v1 of the control as a  function  of concentration shown here in the  bottom panels  so if the ratio of the fraction circled  the compound  is small compared to the control then  um we consider  um we consider the compound to have some  should be showing some inhibition at a  given concentration so for this first  compound  you can see that the ratio at least on  the green plate and the red plate  are very small around point five  and this ratio approaches one at lower  concentrations  for the second compound it looks like  all three plates in the triplicate have  a ratio close to zero  at 10 micromolar um the ratio  is small also at three micromolar and  then approaches one  at higher concentrations for this last  compound  it's a little bit unclear what's  happening at higher concentrations  but there does seem to be a trend  in the fraction sickled of the compound  relative to the control  such it approaches one at lower even  lower concentrations so  we used svd on all of these data and  were able to get these plots  we ended up fitting those those  data and calculating an ac 50 for each  compound  this particular compound we found to be  an inhibitor  at about 10 micromolar this particular  compound  shows inhibition down to three  micromolar  and after looking really hard at the  images  from this compound we've we found that  there was  hemolysis at higher concentrations  but at lower concentrations we found  that this compound  sphered swelled and sphered cells and  and inhibited sickling  but didn't actually lyse the cells so we  consider this compound a lysis hip but  also  it's an inhibitor down to about 30  micromolar  our dose response screen of 335  compounds at 9 concentrations resulted  in 138 hits  at concentrations of 10 micromolar or  less  96 compounds show therapeutically  significant anti-sickling activity  42 compounds caused hemolysis and so in  total  138 compounds were considered hits  and de-anonymized for future testing  this de-anonymization happened this  spring and so  it has opened up a lot of reading and  more experiments for us to do  with that i would like to conclude and  briefly mention some of the future  directions that we're taking  sickle cell disease has been extensively  studied since the 1940s  and it's considered the first molecular  disease unfortunately though there's  only one single partially successful  drug  that can be taken to treat the disease  our research uses the biotech lionheart  automated microscope  and it the microscope allows us to  screen thousands of compounds  in a given week our assay identifies  uh compounds that inhibit polymerization  and sickling which is the cause of the  disease pathology  recent de-anonymization has allowed us  to further test hit compounds in a whole  blood assay  which is an ongoing project in our group  we just started this a couple months ago  not very long ago  and our assay is also being used  currently on mice with sickle trait and  sickle cell disease as well as an  emitted pivot research clinical trial at  nih so  all this is to say we have a lot of  ongoing  experiments with that  i would like to  thank my advisor at nih bill eaton  and my colleagues belle who troy quan  and eric  we each bring something unique to the  table some of us are  physicists who do computer simulations  and theoretical calculations and some of  us are experimentalists  in the lab so i would also um  so we work together to um  to get this we we had to work together  to get the stroke screen off the ground  um i'd also like to thank a couple  people at biotech  diane and mega who have been an  invaluable resources for me  they've they helped a ton in getting  the lion heart just the way we needed to  be to  best work for our essay so with that  i will be happy to take questions  all right well thank you emily for the  informative presentation  uh we'll start the q a portion of the  webinar i just want to remind people  that if you  have have a question that you haven't  asked you can do so right now  if you click on the ask a question box  located on the far left of your screen  uh looks like we have a couple so let's  get started our first question  is why did you use sickle trach blood  for this drug screen  yeah so um i think i briefly mentioned  that we use sickle trait blood  in our drug screen rather than sickle  blood from sickle cell  rather than blood from donors who have  full sickle cell disease  and the reason because um we don't want  someone with anemia to come in week in  and week out to be giving blood  for our essay this is this is a project  that's been  ongoing for um i think  at least a decade now and so at nih  um it's it's been really helpful to  have a steady stream of donors here at  nih who are willing to come  once a week every week for years at a  time to give blood so  we wouldn't want to ask someone with  sickle cell disease to do that  people with sickle trait um don't really  suffer any of the  negative health consequences of those  with sickle cell disease  um in addition to that sickle trait  blood is a little bit less than me  to use in an assay so that's why  we are using sickle trait blood at this  point  um see our next question is do you  expect any differences  in the assay or results when switching  from using sickle trade blood to sickle  cell blood  um the answer is yes so  one of the goals of our in the near  future  is to actually move from using  dilute sickle trait blood to whole blood  and then  moving from whole blood from sickle  trait donors to whole blood from sickle  cell  donors and we do expect some differences  the primary difference is that we expect  blood  from sickle cell donors to sickle much  faster  in our assay there is a lag  in sickling that delay time  is on the order of 100 to 200 minutes or  so  we expect sickle cell blood to sickle  much faster just because  um all of the hemoglobin in sickle cell  blood is hemoglobin s whereas in sickle  trait  blood the proportion of hemoglobin is  about 55  hemoglobin a which doesn't sickle and 35  hemoglobin s  which does sickle  we've got another one here um do any  compounds you test to do anything to  change  the cellular morphology outside of what  you're used to seeing when cells sickle  and how does your machine learning  algorithm account for those changes  um yeah so some of the compounds we  tested  do change the cellular morphology  outside of  um just forming biconcave disk  or sickled cells so i kind of alluded to  that  in my example of the lysis inhibitors  we did notice that that particular  compound  caused cells to sphere immediately  after loading the plate with red blood  cells  we also noticed that some compounds to  cause  some compounds cause crenation in our  red blood cells  so created red blood cells  are are biconcave discs and that they  have a somewhat transparent center but  then they also have  kind of a bumpy surface to them it's  very easy to see  in our in our images when that happens  and um so yeah so we had to  really watch out for that we even though  we use machine  learning to analyze all of our images we  actually do go back and look at just  about  every every single well in  every single plate and so we've looked  at a lot of images  to make sure that the machine learning  algorithm really  and the resulting kinetics curves really  represents what's happening  so our machine learning algorithm  is always a work in progress we are  constantly trying to  make it better and make it such that it  better defines  um the cell shapes that we're seeing and  we currently don't have  a great way to identify  good cells but it's something that we're  constantly working on  let's see we have another one here you  mentioned using this assay to test mouse  blood  can you talk a little bit more about  those experiments  yeah so one project that i was involved  in  last year was  a project where mice were fed either  a normal diet  or an extremely low iron diet  and it's thought that um  in the presence of a low it's not that  mice on a low iron diet  will be less prone to sickle cell  disease so  my collaborators were in charge of the  mice  and feeding the mice and treating the  mice and  then they gave us these these mice were  bred to have  human hemoglobin s in all of their red  blood cells  and so they were  transgenic with human hemoglobin  fed either an iron rich or an iron  depleted diet  we received the blood in our experiment  in our lab  and then we did this same assay where we  loaded the well plate with this mouse  blood and  measured the sickling kinetics and found  slight differences  in the sickling times for mice on the  iron  rich diet versus mice on the iron  depleted diet  all right well looks like we have time  for one more question  what are some of the metrics other than  cell area eccentricity and density ratio  that you're able to measure using your  machine learning assay platform  yeah to be honest  i could not list all 12 of the metrics  that we're currently using  but i know that one of the metrics that  is currently used is the perimeter of  the cell  so the  the machine learning algorithm is um is  set up  to measure the number of pixels along  the edge of the cell  and there's a range in which  there's a um a range in pixel number  that we think  a normal cell a normal biconcave disc  cell  has versus that of a sickled cell versus  that of a crenated cell so you could  imagine if you have  a circular a circular cell by concave  disc  it has x number of pixels on the edge  um that forms the perimeter but a cell  that is created and has lots of little  bumps on the edge  would actually have a larger perimeter a  larger number of pixels making up the  perimeter of the cell  such that that would be a useful metric  for identifying if a cell  is larger than a normal biconcave disk  so um i know the perimeter is one of  them  um i would have to probably look up what  some of the others are so  i hope that created that a little bit  well  thank you for your time today and your  important research  uh before we go i'd like to thank the  audience for joining us  and any questions submitted during the  on-demand period will be addressed  by the speaker via the contact  information that you provided at the  time of your registration  if you have any other questions for  emily today please join her  in the networking area of the virtual  conference starting in a couple minutes  and going um until let's say 2 30 p.m  eastern time if you have any biotech  specific product questions  please visit us at the biotech booth  found in the exhibit hall  this webcast can also be viewed on  demand through july of 2021  so until next time thank you and goodbye


